WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.892
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:08.000
BENJAMIN TREYNOR SLOSS:
So metrics that matter.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.820
The remit for this talk was
basically, all right, fine,

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:12.491
you know you're in charge of--

00:00:12.491 --> 00:00:13.740
oh, I should introduce myself.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.906
So my name is Ben, and if
Google ever stops working,

00:00:15.906 --> 00:00:19.129
it's my fault. I
forgot that part.

00:00:19.129 --> 00:00:20.670
We did the intro in
the last session,

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.120
and I was running with that.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:28.580
More concretely, I created the
Site Reliability team at Google

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:29.900
and still run it.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.640
I also run all of
networking for Google

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.430
and all of data centers
for Google from design

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:36.380
and construction to operation.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:40.790
So it's a low-stress
job, regular hours.

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:42.710
And it teaches you a
thing or two about scale.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:44.460
And my background is
software engineering.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:47.280
So this is sort of the first
operationally oriented gig I've

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.530
ever done in my entire career.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:51.320
It's been an interesting
learning experience,

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.640
but I have learned a
few things about scale.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.790
And one of the
things that I've seen

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:58.160
is sort of the pattern,
over and over again,

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.890
of the same small
number of metrics

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:05.660
show up time after time
in operating and measuring

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:08.360
and assessing software
services that are running

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:09.410
at a decent-sized scale.

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:12.100
And so rather than having a
generic talk about metrics

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:13.310
and why they're
important, blah blah blah,

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:14.570
you already know that, right?

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:15.380
Skip all that.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.879
Let me tell you the three that
I've seen over and over again

00:01:17.879 --> 00:01:20.000
actually are really
important, if you

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.500
want to have a great
user experience

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:22.958
at the end of the
day and you don't

00:01:22.958 --> 00:01:26.278
want to have your service
collapse under its own weight.

00:01:26.278 --> 00:01:31.630
So I've been doing
this since 2003.

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:33.430
Back then we still
had a cloud product.

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:34.929
We didn't call it
cloud at the time.

00:01:34.929 --> 00:01:37.542
We called it Borg, but
it was all of the things

00:01:37.542 --> 00:01:40.000
that you're now familiar with
in the public cloud offering.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:40.840
We had services.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.310
They run in containers.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:45.670
You have these relatively
homogeneous and entirely

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:47.674
fungible compute platforms
that you run it on.

00:01:47.674 --> 00:01:49.840
And jobs can move around
and things can die and blah

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.940
blah blah blah blah.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:54.580
And it was a good
way to run Search,

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.490
and it turned out to be
a good way to run Gmail,

00:01:57.490 --> 00:01:59.350
and we've kind of run with it.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.300
And now, of course,
it's something

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.940
that we're offering to
everyone, and the term cloud

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:04.660
has picked up.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.764
But we've been doing
this for a while.

00:02:06.764 --> 00:02:08.680
And in particular, when
I went back and looked

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:11.320
at the scale of what
Google's been doing,

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.750
we've been through seven
orders of magnitude

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:16.000
so far and still growing.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:16.810
So who knows?

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:18.393
In a couple of years
this presentation

00:02:18.393 --> 00:02:20.110
may read differently.

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:22.090
But we have managed
over that time

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:25.467
to evolve some best practices
about how you run things

00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:26.800
and also how you measure things.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.300
We're going to skip how you run
things for a separate topic.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.270
This is just about measuring.

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:34.420
And again, I want to note
this is not everything

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:35.590
that's important to measure.

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:37.381
These are the things
that I've found really

00:02:37.381 --> 00:02:41.680
are both critical and
often not actually measured

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.900
because they're not
easy to measure.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:46.717
But in retrospect, after
the bad user experience

00:02:46.717 --> 00:02:48.550
or after the outage or
whatever, you sort of

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:50.890
go back and say,
wow, if only we had--

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:54.850
This is the "if
only we had" list.

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:56.130
So item number one--

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:59.350
measure your actual
user experience.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:00.700
Seems straightforward, right?

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:04.570
You've all put your time
and effort and sweat equity

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:08.192
into building this service,
and you test it yourself.

00:03:08.192 --> 00:03:10.150
If you're pretty much
like everybody out there,

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:12.040
your development team
uses your own product.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:15.310
Otherwise, they wouldn't
be working in your company.

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:19.480
But you're testing it here or
in your development lab, close

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:22.420
to your servers, on a
really great network

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:26.430
with fairly little competition
and probably Wi-Fi backup.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.930
So, newsflash--
most of the world

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:30.610
does not experience
your product the way

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.026
you are experiencing
your product.

00:03:32.026 --> 00:03:35.860
In fact, statistically speaking,
very little of your user base

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:37.390
experiences it that way.

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:39.370
So rule number one
that we've come up with

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:43.330
is measure the actual
user experience, right?

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:45.160
Because your users
don't care how well it

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:49.344
works on your awesome brand
new Pixel on your Wi-Fi

00:03:49.344 --> 00:03:50.760
in your home office
with your data

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.530
center 20 milliseconds away.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:54.910
They care how it works for them.

00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:56.455
And if it doesn't
work well for them,

00:03:56.455 --> 00:03:58.330
somebody else will build
a product that does.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:02.870
I know the answer.

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:04.130
But hang on.

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:05.955
But it's hard.

00:04:05.955 --> 00:04:06.455
I apologize.

00:04:06.455 --> 00:04:07.940
I'm going to move this thing.

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:11.360
It's hard to measure the
actual user experience.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:13.520
It's much easier to
measure telemetry that's

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:16.970
coming back to our
servers or to measure

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:19.724
what we see in our test lab.

00:04:19.724 --> 00:04:22.760
I apologize-- speaking of
measuring the actual user

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:23.300
experience.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:25.028
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:25.028 --> 00:04:26.960
It's my first time
with a headset,

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.030
and I think it's not
entirely agreeing with me.

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:35.140
So why do most
teams not actually

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:37.060
measure what their
users are experiencing?

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:40.450
Well, because you can't
exactly talk to your user base

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:42.550
into putting a GoPro behind
their head every time

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:44.966
they're using your product so
you can see what's going on.

00:04:44.966 --> 00:04:48.070
It turns out that has a
very low adoption rate.

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:52.370
And it's not actually easy
to measure, especially

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:55.039
a mobile experience
or, in particular,

00:04:55.039 --> 00:04:56.080
a web browser experience.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:57.670
Back when everything
was thick client,

00:04:57.670 --> 00:04:59.470
you could kind of instrument
your thick client,

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:00.910
and you'd have a
decent sense of what

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:01.990
the user experience was like.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:04.281
But when you have all sorts
of other layers of software

00:05:04.281 --> 00:05:06.410
in between your app and
what the user is doing,

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:07.540
and then you also
have a network,

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.370
and maybe you have a browser,
et cetera, et cetera,

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:10.300
it gets much harder.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:13.030
And so, as a practical
matter, what I see often

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:16.240
is that teams
approximate this, right?

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:19.270
They'll put in some decent
server-side monitoring.

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:21.075
They may even have
a test lab where

00:05:21.075 --> 00:05:23.200
they get some benchmark
results, like how long does

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:24.500
a page take to load and so on.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:26.830
But they aren't actually
measuring the distribution

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:30.070
of what the user experience
is because it's difficult.

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:31.420
My advice for all of you?

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:32.860
Yes, it's hard.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:34.837
Get through that
denial and get on

00:05:34.837 --> 00:05:36.170
to actually measuring the thing.

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:38.980
And if you have to spend 10% of
your engineering team to do it,

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:39.647
do it.

00:05:39.647 --> 00:05:41.980
Because that is what your
user experience is, and you're

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:44.620
spending the other 90%
trying to write a software

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:47.485
service or a feature
or a cool widget,

00:05:47.485 --> 00:05:49.360
and you have no idea
what people actually see

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:51.820
when they try to use it, right?

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:56.140
So it's the number one omission.

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:57.790
What can I say?

00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:00.610
Even at Google, we pretty
regularly run into this.

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:03.610
The team presents, here's what
they think the service is like.

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:07.299
And then you actually go
and do tours, for example,

00:06:07.299 --> 00:06:07.840
and you look.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:10.520
And the experience
is totally different.

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:12.230
We see it over and over again.

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:13.640
I see it when I
talk to companies

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:16.300
that are building products
both on Cloud and not on Cloud.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:20.660
They really only approximate
what the user experience is.

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:24.180
And why is this important?

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:27.140
Well, let me give you a case
study from Google's history.

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:31.300
Ah-- defense in depth--

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:33.720
very important concept
in reliability.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:36.090
We're not going to
talk about it today.

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.910
But you just had it illustrated.

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:42.360
So back in 2004, Google
launched this service.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:43.780
Some of you may
have heard of it.

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:45.360
It's called Gmail.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:49.030
It promised at the time
a gigabyte of storage.

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:51.207
Ooh, ah!

00:06:51.207 --> 00:06:52.790
Back at the time,
that was impressive.

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:54.690
I think you got 5
megabytes on some

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:57.060
of the other popular services.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:00.390
And people's use of it was
almost entirely browser-based.

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:03.510
There was mobile adoption, but
it was not the dominant way

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:04.950
of accessing Gmail.

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:08.130
And so the teams did what
pretty much everybody did, which

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:10.720
is well, let's look at what's
happening at the server, right?

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:13.095
And if we get the HTTP request,
and we send the response,

00:07:13.095 --> 00:07:15.600
and it took us 220
milliseconds on average

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:18.870
to server the response, and we
had an error rate of whatever,

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:23.614
0.007%, life is good.

00:07:23.614 --> 00:07:24.780
So that went on for a while.

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:26.770
We got anecdotal reports
of people saying,

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:28.840
well, you know Gmail is
slow, and the experience

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:32.220
is not really that great,
especially if I'm not

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:38.060
connected to really fast 2
megahertz Wi-Fi, et cetera, et

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:38.770
cetera.

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:43.060
And so eventually we decided
to launch a new Gmail client.

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:44.910
It's JavaScript that
actually got downloaded

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:46.170
when you started using Gmail.

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:49.350
And we decided to
instrument the client.

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:53.010
And then we had the
five stages of grief--

00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:56.210
starting with, as it
always does, denial, right?

00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:59.070
No, no, no, this can't
possibly be right.

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:01.950
There's no way it's taking
20 seconds to load the inbox,

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:05.550
because when I do it here
at Google on my brand new--

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:08.250
oh god, at the time, who knows--

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:14.690
Intel laptop running Firefox,
it's nice and snappy, right?

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:17.154
Well, I don't need to spell
the rest of it out for you.

00:08:17.154 --> 00:08:18.570
That is not the
normal experience.

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:20.340
And in fact, the
inbox load times

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:21.750
were very, very, very slow.

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:24.000
The mail message load
times were very slow.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.410
In some cases, the page
render times were very slow.

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:27.660
You had to get through denial.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:30.810
So after a couple of rounds of
making sure the metrics were

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:33.480
right and actually doing
tests out in the field,

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:35.789
we convinced ourselves,
OK, actually no, this

00:08:35.789 --> 00:08:37.380
is what the user experience is.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:39.654
Wow, that's pretty crappy.

00:08:39.654 --> 00:08:41.820
And we got eventually to
bargaining, which basically

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:43.440
became a whole new
set of changes,

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:46.500
both interestingly changes
on the server side, things

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:48.270
like delivering
incremental updates

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:50.220
and delivering
real-time time feed,

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:51.720
and obviously on
the client side,

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:54.210
where you make rendering
changes and you

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:56.522
change sort of how the page
is presented and so on.

00:08:56.522 --> 00:08:58.230
You actually might
present it differently

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:00.290
on a different-speed
device so that you

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:02.790
don't consume too much of your
time just trying to literally

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:05.520
draw the pixels on the screen.

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:07.140
And I remember distinctly--

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:11.730
this was 2006-ish, there
was this inflection.

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:13.380
Gmail had been
growing beautifully.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:15.300
It had an
up-and-to-the-right curve,

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:17.340
which I will now
illustrate in the air.

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:18.840
And then we launched
the new client,

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:20.730
and then its curve did that.

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:22.410
And it was this--
you could correlate

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.690
this change in
the adoption curve

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:27.750
to us launching the new client
and the server updates that

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:30.720
were entirely due to having
actually measured the user

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:34.900
experience and getting through
the five stages of grief.

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:36.120
It was a significant effort.

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:38.760
There was a lot of debate at the
time about, is this important?

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:40.884
And shouldn't we be adding
a bunch of new features?

00:09:40.884 --> 00:09:43.920
And look at Hotmail and look
at Yahoo Mail and et cetera.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:45.870
But we spent the effort
to improve the client

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:47.744
and to improve the user
experience for people

00:09:47.744 --> 00:09:50.460
who didn't have
the latest laptop

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:52.800
and didn't have the really
fast Wi-Fi and so on.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:55.200
And it made this enormous
difference in actual adoption.

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:58.471
So for me, that cemented the
lesson for forever, right?

00:09:58.471 --> 00:10:00.720
Measure the user experience,
even though it's painful,

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:03.060
even though it's going to take
a bunch of big engineering

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:05.559
projects, because you will never
build your app the same way

00:10:05.559 --> 00:10:08.160
again, and you will get a
much better user adoption

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:10.530
as a result of doing it--
much more impactful than any

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:12.817
feature that we
could have launched.

00:10:12.817 --> 00:10:13.900
And yes, it's hard, right?

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:15.370
So the engineering
team will also

00:10:15.370 --> 00:10:17.230
have to go through the
five stages of grief

00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:18.610
in dealing with the
fact that in addition

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:19.900
to writing all the
features, they also

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:21.190
need to write the things
that let you measure

00:10:21.190 --> 00:10:22.653
what the users are seeing.

00:10:22.653 --> 00:10:23.599
But persist.

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:28.830
Metric number
two-- tail latency.

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:30.176
All right.

00:10:30.176 --> 00:10:31.800
Sounds incredibly
boring, unless you're

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:35.160
in reliability, in which
case we live for this stuff.

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:38.850
But let's run with boring
for right now, right?

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:41.580
So a quick survey of
the room-- hands up

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:45.320
if you think roughly you know
how reliable your service is.

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:48.459
Ah, see, I gave
way too much away.

00:10:48.459 --> 00:10:50.250
The only people who
are raising their hands

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:55.052
are the ones who clearly do not
know [LAUGHS] because they're

00:10:55.052 --> 00:10:57.510
in my team and they already
know what this drill is, right?

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:02.400
So yes, we just talked
about the importance

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:04.920
of actually measuring the
user experience the way

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:06.810
the users experience it.

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:08.850
But if you do that,
then clearly you

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:10.782
know how often the
user gets an error.

00:11:10.782 --> 00:11:12.990
And therefore, you know how
often your user considers

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:15.674
your service to be available.

00:11:15.674 --> 00:11:18.090
If that describes you and your
perspective on your service

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:23.110
right now, this will come as
something of a disappointment.

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:28.350
So users typically treat a
sufficiently slow response

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:30.100
as the same thing as
an error, and they'll

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:31.000
do one of two things.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:35.560
They'll hit refresh if they're
kind and otherwise think highly

00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:36.389
of your app.

00:11:36.389 --> 00:11:37.930
Or they'll switch
to a different app,

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:39.400
and maybe they'll
come back to yours.

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:41.410
But certainly they won't
have a good experience,

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:42.700
and it will be the
same for them as

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:45.160
if they had gotten an error,
except the error at least is

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:48.070
sort of a polite prompt that
says you should hit Refresh,

00:11:48.070 --> 00:11:53.940
whereas no answer is impolite
and tells you to you your time.

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:57.140
And so I think of availability
as being the above formula.

00:11:57.140 --> 00:12:02.270
Availability is 100% minus all
of the errors that you return

00:12:02.270 --> 00:12:05.870
and also minus all of the
slow responses you give.

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:07.820
And if you picture
this for yourself--

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:11.870
suppose you use a nice, snappy,
modern web-based application

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:14.090
or mobile-based application--

00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:16.910
we offer a few of
them ourselves.

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:20.024
And on average you make a
query, and within half a second

00:12:20.024 --> 00:12:22.190
the thing responds and
actually gives you the answer

00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:23.540
or does the transition
or whatever.

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:24.039
Fine.

00:12:24.039 --> 00:12:25.340
That's great.

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:28.880
So suppose one time
in 30, whatever

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:31.010
it was that it was
doing to process that,

00:12:31.010 --> 00:12:33.530
fails somewhere
internally in your stack,

00:12:33.530 --> 00:12:35.660
and so eventually your
time-outs kick in,

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:37.789
and you assume-- you
retry the query for them,

00:12:37.789 --> 00:12:39.330
as opposed to making
them hit Refresh

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:42.980
because that's barbaric.

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:45.590
So you refresh after 10
seconds, and it retries.

00:12:45.590 --> 00:12:46.190
OK.

00:12:46.190 --> 00:12:48.800
So your availability
metrics there

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:51.804
will say that it's 99.9%
something available,

00:12:51.804 --> 00:12:53.720
because that's how many
errors you're serving.

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:56.210
Your users will
say it's about 97%

00:12:56.210 --> 00:13:00.500
available, which is for a
mobile app not terrible,

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:01.670
but it's not great, right?

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:03.086
Most of the mobile
apps that I use

00:13:03.086 --> 00:13:05.060
are probably on the
order of 99% available,

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:07.190
and I can definitely
notice as a user

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:09.500
the difference between
let's say 99.5%

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:12.927
and 97%, which is
what I have here.

00:13:12.927 --> 00:13:14.510
You'd have a completely
different view

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:16.820
of what the user
experience is just

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:19.220
by changing the way that
you consider availability.

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:21.020
And so then the
question, of course,

00:13:21.020 --> 00:13:24.060
becomes well, how much
slower than median

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:26.060
does the thing have to
be before the user will

00:13:26.060 --> 00:13:29.030
consider it to be an error?

00:13:29.030 --> 00:13:30.860
I don't have a
generic answer for you

00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:33.330
because it depends
a lot on your app.

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:36.170
If you're using an
app like Mail and it

00:13:36.170 --> 00:13:38.660
takes a few extra seconds
for a mail to be received,

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:40.310
you don't even notice.

00:13:40.310 --> 00:13:42.050
If it takes a few
extra seconds for you

00:13:42.050 --> 00:13:45.080
to hit send before the mail
is registered as being sent,

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:46.890
you notice that, right?

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:49.550
And if you click on a mail
and the mail doesn't snap up

00:13:49.550 --> 00:13:51.560
instantly, you're going
to try it yourself

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:54.290
in a couple of seconds,
so maybe factor of four.

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:57.920
But you can probably see
this, and if you can't, you

00:13:57.920 --> 00:13:59.076
should figure it out.

00:13:59.076 --> 00:14:00.950
Again, back to measuring
the user experience,

00:14:00.950 --> 00:14:03.616
because you want to know at what
point your user thinks your app

00:14:03.616 --> 00:14:05.570
isn't working correctly.

00:14:05.570 --> 00:14:08.780
And if you don't know
that, well, your users do,

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:10.820
and you won't change
your app appropriately,

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:13.670
and then you'll have less
adoption and eventually

00:14:13.670 --> 00:14:16.100
catastrophe.

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:19.610
So if you want a rule of thumb,
something to walk away with,

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:20.660
5x.

00:14:20.660 --> 00:14:22.190
5x is a pretty
good rule of thumb

00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:24.200
for many interactive
applications

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:26.180
that I see people using.

00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:28.850
And it's not complicated
at that point, right?

00:14:28.850 --> 00:14:31.782
You can measure what
your median latency is.

00:14:31.782 --> 00:14:33.990
Just consider anything that's
more than five times as

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:38.280
slow as your median latency
to be an error, and again--

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:41.340
five stages of grief, followed
by then you'll actually

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:43.920
spend some time and try to
figure out really is it 5x,

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:45.900
or is it more like 8x?

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:48.690
I encourage you not to be
overly optimistic because again,

00:14:48.690 --> 00:14:51.032
eventually the users will
make their own choice

00:14:51.032 --> 00:14:51.990
about your application.

00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:53.640
You'd really like to
know what they're seeing

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:55.098
and how they're
behaving, and you'd

00:14:55.098 --> 00:14:57.090
like to have the
opportunity to tune your app

00:14:57.090 --> 00:14:58.680
to deal with that.

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:03.874
But figure out what latency
starts to cause people to retry

00:15:03.874 --> 00:15:05.790
or starts to cause them
to switch off your app

00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:08.070
and do something different
for a while and come back,

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:11.490
and tune yourself so that your
tail latency is below that.

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:13.350
And tune your metrics
so that once you

00:15:13.350 --> 00:15:16.870
hit that tail latency that
causes them to switch behavior,

00:15:16.870 --> 00:15:18.720
you consider it an
error, and then you

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:21.060
can decide how available
you want your app to be.

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:23.700
But that is actually measuring
the user's experience

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:26.490
of availability and not
your server's experience

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:28.110
of availability.

00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:31.320
And if you haven't measured the
sort of tail latency before,

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:33.150
like I said, just
five stages of grief.

00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:34.270
Let it happen, right?

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:36.660
You'll get through it.

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:39.980
But don't stay in denial.

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:43.100
So topic number three then
is service efficiency.

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:45.810
And this could mean a
lot of different things,

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:48.812
but it is the place that I
see the most applications get

00:15:48.812 --> 00:15:50.270
tripped up on, so
I wanted to spend

00:15:50.270 --> 00:15:51.900
a bit of time talking about it.

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:53.510
So let's talk about
capacity planning.

00:15:53.510 --> 00:15:57.830
It is as boring and dry a
topic as you could possibly

00:15:57.830 --> 00:16:00.134
imagine for most developers--

00:16:00.134 --> 00:16:01.550
pretty much almost
all developers,

00:16:01.550 --> 00:16:03.215
I think, unless you're
actually charged

00:16:03.215 --> 00:16:05.090
with making sure the
app works, in which case

00:16:05.090 --> 00:16:07.370
it is near and
dear to your heart.

00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:10.700
So capacity planning is
essentially a simple thing.

00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:13.262
You take demand,
which you typically

00:16:13.262 --> 00:16:14.470
get from historical behavior.

00:16:14.470 --> 00:16:18.020
You extrapolate it
forward through time.

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:20.397
You add in anything
that we call inorganic,

00:16:20.397 --> 00:16:22.730
but basically if you're going
to change your application

00:16:22.730 --> 00:16:25.340
to make the demand go up,
or issue two queries instead

00:16:25.340 --> 00:16:27.287
of one, or whatever
you're going to do

00:16:27.287 --> 00:16:29.120
that changes your demand
curve that you know

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:32.600
is going to happen,
you add it in there.

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:34.790
You divide that by
how many servers

00:16:34.790 --> 00:16:36.590
it takes to actually
answer a query,

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:40.310
and there again, you can change
the server, blah blah blah.

00:16:40.310 --> 00:16:42.689
But it's probably been
fairly constant over time.

00:16:42.689 --> 00:16:45.230
So you divide that and it tells
you how many servers you need

00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:47.540
or how many instances you
need or, if you happen

00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:49.430
to be stuck on a cloud product
that makes you buy stuff

00:16:49.430 --> 00:16:51.388
in advance to get a good
price, how many you're

00:16:51.388 --> 00:16:53.900
going to have to purchase,
and you're done, right?

00:16:53.900 --> 00:16:56.300
Easy peasy.

00:16:56.300 --> 00:16:58.860
Well, see, no,
unfortunately, because there

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:02.460
is this extra bit, which is that
you're all either developers

00:17:02.460 --> 00:17:05.325
in this room or you manage
developers for the most part.

00:17:05.325 --> 00:17:08.460
All of you are doing things
to that binary that's

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:09.552
running on those servers.

00:17:09.552 --> 00:17:11.010
And you're doing
things that change

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:12.990
the amount of time
that is required

00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:14.880
for processing a given query.

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:17.069
And so even if you are
perfect at extrapolating

00:17:17.069 --> 00:17:19.319
how many queries you're
going to be getting,

00:17:19.319 --> 00:17:22.510
you're changing, if you
will, the denominator.

00:17:22.510 --> 00:17:25.470
And sometimes you're not
changing it by a little bit.

00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:27.180
It was not uncommon
for us to see

00:17:27.180 --> 00:17:30.685
swings of 50% over
the course of a week

00:17:30.685 --> 00:17:32.310
if you're not watching
it, because it's

00:17:32.310 --> 00:17:34.080
easy to sort of add
things to the inner loop.

00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:36.246
And it's easy to add a
feature that makes a call out

00:17:36.246 --> 00:17:38.430
to something else
and blah blah blah.

00:17:38.430 --> 00:17:41.370
And even small changes, like
configuration file changes,

00:17:41.370 --> 00:17:42.949
can sometimes
dramatically change

00:17:42.949 --> 00:17:44.490
the amount of compute
capacity that's

00:17:44.490 --> 00:17:46.560
required to process a query.

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:48.900
And we call that service
efficiency overall, right?

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:52.950
How many CPU cycles,
disk spindle seeks,

00:17:52.950 --> 00:17:54.950
network bytes, et
cetera, et cetera,

00:17:54.950 --> 00:17:58.320
is required to answer a query or
to serve an interesting action

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:00.330
in your service.

00:18:00.330 --> 00:18:03.665
And, like I said, easy to
change, basically invisible,

00:18:03.665 --> 00:18:05.040
unless you do
performance testing

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:06.915
all the time, which
we'll get to in a second.

00:18:06.915 --> 00:18:10.440
And one of the most frequent
flyers in the post-mortem that

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:12.660
follows inevitably
from this, because

00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:14.580
of course what happens
with service efficiency

00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:18.270
is none of you, presumably,
run your services

00:18:18.270 --> 00:18:20.700
at the very hairy edge of
falling over all the time.

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:23.610
Everyone builds redundancy
into how they do provisioning.

00:18:23.610 --> 00:18:26.480
Either it's redundancy to handle
peaks that happen to happen.

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:29.250
You're running a news service,
and something interesting

00:18:29.250 --> 00:18:30.870
happens in the world.

00:18:30.870 --> 00:18:34.450
You can use your own imagination
as to what that might be.

00:18:34.450 --> 00:18:38.040
And demand spikes 30%.

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:40.950
Or you're running a
photo application,

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:44.360
and there's a concert, and so
within this one area within,

00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:45.960
this one geographic
region, suddenly

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:48.000
the number of pictures people
are taking-- no, you don't even

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:48.708
need that, right?

00:18:48.708 --> 00:18:51.180
Just wait for New Year's Eve,
and the number of pictures

00:18:51.180 --> 00:18:53.550
that get taken on
these hourly spikes.

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:56.824
It's very odd,
right, goes way up.

00:18:56.824 --> 00:18:58.740
So you have enough
redundancy to handle spikes

00:18:58.740 --> 00:19:01.090
and also to handle a
certain amount of failure.

00:19:01.090 --> 00:19:04.530
Service efficiency changes
eat into that capacity reserve

00:19:04.530 --> 00:19:06.330
that you've got, but
they do it invisibly

00:19:06.330 --> 00:19:07.530
because normally
you're provisioned

00:19:07.530 --> 00:19:08.560
to have a bit of failure.

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:10.140
And if you don't have a
bit of failure happening

00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:12.810
at the same time you have a
spike, everything runs great,

00:19:12.810 --> 00:19:13.950
right?

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:16.200
And so the post-mortem
usually reads something like,

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:18.510
we thought we were
provisioned n plus 2 for peak,

00:19:18.510 --> 00:19:20.520
but it turned out that
over the past two weeks,

00:19:20.520 --> 00:19:23.790
our service efficiency had gone
down because reasons, which

00:19:23.790 --> 00:19:25.650
could be like we
introduced a new CPU

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:28.310
type that we were using, or
somebody checked something in.

00:19:28.310 --> 00:19:30.090
The list of reasons
is always different.

00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:31.806
The result is always the same.

00:19:31.806 --> 00:19:33.180
So it turned out
we were actually

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:35.346
provisioned only n plus 5,
and then one of our sites

00:19:35.346 --> 00:19:38.430
had an outage, and
then cascade failure.

00:19:38.430 --> 00:19:40.600
because once you're
out at a capacity,

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:42.690
unless you've done a very
careful job of designing

00:19:42.690 --> 00:19:46.140
a distributed service to
do very good load shedding,

00:19:46.140 --> 00:19:47.640
cascade failure all
the way through.

00:19:47.640 --> 00:19:50.306
And then the only way to recover
from that is basically shut off

00:19:50.306 --> 00:19:52.950
all the traffic, and then
bleed a little bit back in

00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:54.330
to get the caches
warmed back up,

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:56.370
and eventually you can get back
to a stable operating model,

00:19:56.370 --> 00:19:57.570
but it takes hours.

00:19:57.570 --> 00:19:59.090
And your users notice.

00:19:59.090 --> 00:20:00.210
Ask me how I know this.

00:20:00.210 --> 00:20:01.950
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:05.832
So the best practice
here is-- again

00:20:05.832 --> 00:20:08.290
sorry for creating more work,
but this is really important.

00:20:08.290 --> 00:20:10.380
You've got to measure how
efficient your service actually

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:11.490
is at servicing queries.

00:20:11.490 --> 00:20:15.594
And I don't actually think
that daily is too frequently.

00:20:15.594 --> 00:20:17.010
Best practice, of
course, would be

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:19.230
we'll build a system that
actually measures it for me

00:20:19.230 --> 00:20:21.000
and spits out a
report every night.

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:22.380
And then I'll
notice immediately.

00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:24.338
It's much easier to
automate this sort of thing

00:20:24.338 --> 00:20:25.560
and have a performance test.

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:27.934
And you don't have to test
every little edge case, right?

00:20:27.934 --> 00:20:30.570
You just have to test the
things that are the big volume

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:31.612
drivers for your service.

00:20:31.612 --> 00:20:33.820
But if you have it automated,
and you get this report

00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:34.989
every night, you'll notice.

00:20:34.989 --> 00:20:36.780
You'll also notice
other interesting things

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:38.946
like, hey, our service
efficiency is still the same,

00:20:38.946 --> 00:20:40.500
but our latency has
been creeping up

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:42.434
a few milliseconds every week.

00:20:42.434 --> 00:20:44.100
So we've gone from
having a service that

00:20:44.100 --> 00:20:47.820
was nice and snappy to a
service that's kind of kludgy,

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:50.460
even on a good device, and
really bad on a bad device,

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:52.650
over the course of three
months without anything

00:20:52.650 --> 00:20:53.880
dramatic happening.

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:56.700
And that's obviously something
else that can bite you.

00:20:56.700 --> 00:20:58.260
It bites you mostly
in that your user

00:20:58.260 --> 00:21:00.270
adoption seems to nose
over a bit more than you

00:21:00.270 --> 00:21:03.160
would have hoped.

00:21:03.160 --> 00:21:06.220
If you're running hypothetically
on a cloud provider that just

00:21:06.220 --> 00:21:10.090
has flexible use and so
you can use more capacity,

00:21:10.090 --> 00:21:12.340
you might not have a cascade
failure when your service

00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:14.650
efficiency gets bad,
but what will happen

00:21:14.650 --> 00:21:17.380
is your finance person will come
and find you a few weeks later,

00:21:17.380 --> 00:21:20.920
and they'll say, huh, we got a
bill from our cloud provider,

00:21:20.920 --> 00:21:23.800
and it's about 30% higher
than we had expected.

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:25.450
What's going on?

00:21:25.450 --> 00:21:27.790
And then I would say
typically two or three

00:21:27.790 --> 00:21:29.014
weeks of thrashing goes on.

00:21:29.014 --> 00:21:31.180
You accuse of the cloud
provider of overbilling you.

00:21:31.180 --> 00:21:33.390
The cloud provider does
their own diagnostic

00:21:33.390 --> 00:21:35.235
and says it seems to
be running normally.

00:21:35.235 --> 00:21:37.360
This is assuming you have
a cloud provider who lets

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:38.530
you flex in and out, right?

00:21:38.530 --> 00:21:40.180
If you have one where you're
running on fixed instances,

00:21:40.180 --> 00:21:41.740
then your service
just goes down,

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:43.870
which has the upside
of no extra bill

00:21:43.870 --> 00:21:47.020
and sort of prompt
diagnosis, so I

00:21:47.020 --> 00:21:49.000
guess there's pros
and cons to each side.

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:49.607
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:49.607 --> 00:21:51.190
Personally I'd rather
have the finance

00:21:51.190 --> 00:21:52.815
team yelling at me
than the sales team,

00:21:52.815 --> 00:21:55.690
but you know that's me.

00:21:55.690 --> 00:21:57.400
So to keep your
costs under control,

00:21:57.400 --> 00:21:59.090
but more importantly to
keep your service stable,

00:21:59.090 --> 00:22:01.214
you've got to know what
your service efficiency is.

00:22:01.214 --> 00:22:02.830
And the way to know that--

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:04.600
because developers
will check stuff in,

00:22:04.600 --> 00:22:06.291
that's what you pay them for--

00:22:06.291 --> 00:22:08.290
the way to know that is
to actually benchmark it

00:22:08.290 --> 00:22:10.120
quite regularly.

00:22:10.120 --> 00:22:12.100
So that's actually it.

00:22:12.100 --> 00:22:14.980
Like, if I trace most of
the big post-mortems where

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:17.500
things that were not sort
of obvious were happening,

00:22:17.500 --> 00:22:19.666
I could put them into one
of those three categories.

00:22:19.666 --> 00:22:23.116
And so if you measure
those three metrics

00:22:23.116 --> 00:22:24.490
and go through
the work that it's

00:22:24.490 --> 00:22:26.573
going to take to actually
measure them accurately,

00:22:26.573 --> 00:22:30.760
not as an approximation, I
think you will find that A,

00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:34.540
you service runs better, B,
you have fewer outages, and C,

00:22:34.540 --> 00:22:37.220
you have a lot
more user adoption.

00:22:37.220 --> 00:22:40.310
And most of us kind of like
those three properties.

00:22:40.310 --> 00:22:41.810
So that is it.

00:22:41.810 --> 00:22:45.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

